[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Strings: Regular Expressions"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Regular expressions (regex or regexp) are powerful tools for pattern matching in strings. They provide a concise and flexible way to search, validate, and manipulate text. This tutorial will cover the fundamentals of regular expressions and their application in data structures and algorithms."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic Regular Expression Syntax"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Regular expressions consist of literal characters and metacharacters. Literal characters match themselves, while metacharacters have special meanings."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":". (dot): Matches any single character except newline."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"^ (caret): Matches the beginning of the string."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"$ (dollar): Matches the end of the string."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"* (asterisk): Matches zero or more occurrences of the preceding character or group."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"+ (plus): Matches one or more occurrences of the preceding character or group."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"? (question mark): Matches zero or one occurrence of the preceding character or group."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"[] (square brackets): Defines a character class, matching any single character within the brackets. For example, [abc] matches 'a', 'b', or 'c'."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"[^] (negated square brackets): Matches any single character NOT within the brackets. For example, [^abc] matches any character except 'a', 'b', or 'c'."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"| (pipe): Acts as an "or" operator, matching either the expression before or after the pipe."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"() (parentheses): Groups parts of the regular expression together."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"\ (backslash): Escapes a metacharacter to treat it as a literal character. For example, \. matches a literal dot."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Character Classes and Quantifiers"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Character classes and quantifiers provide more specific matching capabilities."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"\d: Matches any digit (0-9)."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"\D: Matches any non-digit character."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"\w: Matches any word character (a-z, A-Z, 0-9, and _)."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"\W: Matches any non-word character."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"\s: Matches any whitespace character (space, tab, newline)."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"\S: Matches any non-whitespace character."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"{n}: Matches exactly n occurrences of the preceding character or group."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"{n,}: Matches n or more occurrences of the preceding character or group."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"{n,m}: Matches between n and m occurrences of the preceding character or group."},{"order":27,"type":"subheading","text":"Regular Expression Operations"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"Common operations using regular expressions include:"},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Matching: Checking if a string matches a given pattern."},{"order":30,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Searching: Finding the first occurrence of a pattern in a string."},{"order":31,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Replacing: Replacing occurrences of a pattern with another string."},{"order":32,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Splitting: Splitting a string into an array of substrings based on a pattern."},{"order":33,"type":"subheading","text":"Examples"},{"order":34,"type":"code","text":"// Example: Matching an email address
const emailRegex = /^[\w-\.]+@([\w-]+\.)+[\w-]{2,4}$/;
const email = "test@example.com";
const isValid = emailRegex.test(email);
console.log(isValid); // Output: true"},{"order":35,"type":"code","text":"// Example: Extracting numbers from a string
const text = "There are 123 apples and 456 oranges.";
const numberRegex = /\d+/g;
const numbers = text.match(numberRegex);
console.log(numbers); // Output: ["123", "456"]"},{"order":36,"type":"code","text":"// Example: Replacing all spaces with underscores
const text = "This is a test string";
const newText = text.replace(/\s+/g, "_");
console.log(newText); // Output: This_is_a_test_string"},{"order":37,"type":"subheading","text":"Regular Expressions in Algorithms"},{"order":38,"type":"paragraph","text":"Regular expressions can be used in various algorithms, such as:"},{"order":39,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Data validation: Validating user input, such as email addresses, phone numbers, and dates."},{"order":40,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Text parsing: Extracting specific information from text files or web pages."},{"order":41,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Search algorithms: Implementing advanced search functionalities with pattern matching."},{"order":42,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Lexical analysis: Breaking down source code into tokens for compilers and interpreters."},{"order":43,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":44,"type":"paragraph","text":"Regular expressions are a valuable tool for string manipulation and pattern matching. Understanding the basic syntax and operations allows you to solve a wide range of problems in data structures and algorithms efficiently. Mastering regular expressions can significantly improve your ability to process and analyze text data."}]
